All references verified, gap confirmed. Here is my review.

```json
{
  "verdict": "pass",
  "scores": {
    "novelty": {
      "score": 4,
      "rationale": "The gap is independently verified as genuine: no existing work connects spectral health monitoring to budget-constrained adaptive global real/synthetic data mixing during recursive training. DCES (confirmed via MDPI) operates at the within-pool sample selection level, not global mixing ratio control. The golden ratio work is static. ADAPT addresses multi-task domain mixing, not collapse. DoGraph (arXiv 2604.07963, Apr 2026 — not cited but not competing) uses gradient-based mixing for general pretraining. The individual components are standard, but the integration is novel and well-motivated."
    },
    "feasibility": {
      "score": 4,
      "rationale": "~100-135 H100-hours is comfortably within the 200-hour budget. 14-day timeline is tight but plausible for a short paper with a well-specified experimental plan. SIGMA reimplementation risk is real but mitigated by the RankMe fallback (verified: ICML 2023, implementations exist). Pythia 410M/1B baselines independently verified (HellaSwag 40.9%/~49.7%, MMLU 27.3% — correctly excluded). Paired seed methodology verified (arXiv 2512.24145 is real). The only concern is whether 5 generations produce a large enough effect, but this is honestly acknowledged as a limitation."
    },
    "clarity": {
      "score": 4,
      "rationale": "The proposal is exceptionally well-specified for a short paper plan: controller formula given in full (r_{t+1}^raw = B_rem/(T-t) + k·max(0, Δ_t - τ) with clipping and budget update), budget-fixed experimental design eliminates confounds, contingency plans for every identified risk (SIGMA transfer, calibration parity, small effect sizes), and statistical power limitations honestly framed. The calibration-parity contingency is particularly well thought through."
    },
    "impact": {
      "score": 4,
      "rationale": "Strong alignment with EMNLP 2026 special theme on data contamination and synthetic data feedback loops. The framework contribution (signal-agnostic closed-loop adaptive mixing) is reusable and extendable. Even modest preliminary results establish a new paradigm (diagnosis → intervention). The budget-fixed redistribution design makes the contribution maximally interpretable. Appropriate impact level for Findings short paper; full impact requires the extended version with more generations and model scales."
    }
  },
  "issues": [
    "MINOR — 5 generations may underexploit the non-linearity motivation: SIGMA's dramatic acceleration occurs around generation 20, so 5 generations may only capture the relatively linear early phase of collapse, yielding a modest effect size for spectral-guided vs. static mixing. The proposal acknowledges this honestly but should explicitly note in the paper that the 5-generation window is a conservative test of the non-linearity hypothesis — success here would be a lower bound on the framework's advantage.",
    "MINOR — Missing related work: DoGraph (arXiv 2604.07963, Apr 2026) proposes gradient-based data mixing optimization via graph-constrained reweighting during LLM pretraining. While it targets general domain mixing rather than synthetic data collapse, it represents concurrent work on adaptive, signal-driven data mixing that should be briefly acknowledged in the related work section.",
    "MINOR — The calibration-parity contingency is well-reasoned, but the paper should also consider the scenario where NEITHER spectral-guided nor calibration-guided mixing significantly outperforms fixed 50/50 at 5 generations. With 3 seeds and potentially small effect sizes, this null-result scenario should be planned for — the paper could still contribute the framework specification and characterization of spectral vs. calibration signal properties, but the framing would need adjustment."
  ],
  "feedback": "The proposal has matured into a well-specified, feasible, and genuinely novel research plan after 5 rounds of thorough revision. All previously identified critical issues have been comprehensively addressed. Three minor suggestions for the paper itself: (1) Frame the 5-generation experiment as a conservative lower-bound test — if dynamic mixing helps even over this short window where collapse pressure is relatively linear, the advantage should be larger at scale. (2) Add DoGraph (arXiv 2604.07963) to related work on adaptive data mixing — it's concurrent work on gradient-based domain mixing that helps position your spectral-guided approach. (3) Prepare a null-result narrative: if 5 generations don't show a statistically significant advantage for dynamic mixing at 3 seeds, the paper can still contribute the closed-loop framework, the spectral/calibration signal comparison, and the budget-constrained controller specification as infrastructure for future work at larger scale.",
  "reason": "Independently verified gap (spectral diagnosis → adaptive budget-constrained mixing), feasible within constraints (100-135 H100-hours, 14 days), well-specified experimental design with honest acknowledgment of limitations — solid EMNLP 2026 Findings short paper after 5 rounds of substantive revision."
